WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.247
Vectorization is one of the central ideas in NumPy.

00:00:03.247 --> 00:00:07.077
Instead of writing a loop that processes one value at a time,

00:00:07.077 --> 00:00:12.267
we describe an operation that applies to an entire array.

00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:14.062
In this example, we have a group of grades.

00:00:14.062 --> 00:00:19.897
We add five points to every grade with one expression.

00:00:19.897 --> 00:00:24.147
Then we use N P dot minimum to make sure no grade goes above one hundred.

00:00:24.147 --> 00:00:29.567
Click Run and compare the original grades with the curved grades.

00:00:29.567 --> 00:00:31.202
Notice that we never wrote a for loop.

00:00:31.202 --> 00:00:35.072
NumPy applies the arithmetic across the whole array.

00:00:35.072 --> 00:00:39.982
This style is usually easier to read for numerical problems,

00:00:39.982 --> 00:00:42.242
and NumPy can perform these operations efficiently.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:49.112
In analytics, vectorization is useful for adjustments, unit conversions, scaling, revenue calculations,

00:00:49.112 --> 00:00:54.912
percentages, feature transformations, and many other repetitive numerical tasks.

00:00:54.912 --> 00:01:01.272
When a calculation naturally applies to every element, think first about whether an array operation can

00:01:01.272 --> 00:01:02.686
express the idea directly.
